		aliases: ["pf1e","pathfinder 1e"],
		body: "# Pathfinder 1E\n\n## What is specific to this system\n\n- Base attack bonus, CMB and CMD, and three separate saves (Fortitude, Reflex, Will)\n  rather than PF2E's proficiency ladder.\n- Iterative attacks at high BAB rather than a multiple attack penalty.\n- Alignment is in play and mechanically load-bearing for some classes.\n\n## In combat\n\n- Standard, move, swift and full-round actions. A full attack is a full-round action, so\n  moving usually costs the extra attacks.\n- Attacks of opportunity are frequent; provoke deliberately and tell the player when\n  they are provoking.\n- Track it explicitly in your message: the action economy is the interesting part.\n\n## Companions and sidekicks\n\nThe action economy is the reason to have one. A lone character facing three enemies is giving up\nthree turns a round to its one, and iterative attacks only help if it can afford to\nstand still.\n\nThe system already ships several ways to add a body without a second full sheet:\nan animal companion, a familiar, or a cohort through the leadership rules for a player who\nwants a genuine second combatant. Prefer those to a bespoke sidekick, because they are\nbalanced against the encounter maths already.\n\nWhere a full ally is wanted, use a creature two or three levels below the party and\ngive it a single signature action. Keep its turn to one or two actions in practice --\nnot because the rules say so, but because a companion taking a full three-action turn\nevery round doubles the length of every fight.\n\nCompanions provoke and are provoked like anyone else, and a companion caught flat-footed\nis a good moment to let the fiction bite.",
