		body: "# Pathfinder 2E — legacy\n\nThis is the printing in play. State it in one clause when you confirm the setup, and do not drift to the other one.\n\n## What is specific to this printing\n\n- The 2019 Core Rulebook. Alignment is in play, schools of magic exist, damage is\n  negative/positive, and spells have their original names.\n- Chosen deliberately over the remaster, so do not quietly modernise a rule.\n\n## In combat\n\n- Three actions per turn, plus a reaction. Most things cost one action; casting usually\n  costs two.\n- **Multiple attack penalty:** second attack in a turn is at -5, third at -10 (-4/-8 with\n  agile). This is the main reason PF2E turns are interesting; enforce it.\n- **Four degrees of success:** beat the DC by 10 for a critical success, fail by 10 for a\n  critical failure. A natural 20 improves the degree by one step, a natural 1 worsens it.\n  The dice tool reports natural 20s and 1s, so you can apply this without guessing.\n- Dying/Wounded: dying increases with each hit while down; wounded makes the next one\n  worse. Track both in the Status block.\n- Hero points: one at the start, more for good play. Spending one rerolls a check;\n  spending all of them recovers from dying. Remind the player they have them.\n\n## Companions and sidekicks\n\nThe action economy is the reason to have one. Three actions each means a lone character\nfacing three enemies is giving up nine actions a round to its three, and the multiple\nattack penalty means it cannot simply swing more.\n\nBoth printings already ship several ways to add a body without a second full sheet:\nan animal companion, a familiar with the right abilities, or an eidolon for a player who\nwants a genuine second combatant. Prefer those to a bespoke sidekick, because they are\nbalanced against the encounter maths already.\n\nWhere a full ally is wanted, use a creature two or three levels below the party and\ngive it a single signature action. Keep its turn to one or two actions in practice --\nnot because the rules say so, but because a companion taking a full three-action turn\nevery round doubles the length of every fight.\n\nDegrees of success apply to companions exactly as to anyone else, and a critical\nfailure on a companion's strike is a good moment to let the fiction bite.",
